    "Stem": "During the audit of Harbor Paper Products for the year ended December 31, the engagement team identified three events occurring between January 1 and February 15 (the financial statement issuance date): (1) A major customer filed for bankruptcy on January 8 — the $285,000 receivable was confirmed fully collectible at December 31; (2) Harbor's main warehouse suffered fire damage of $420,000 on January 22; (3) A lawsuit settlement for $175,000 was reached on February 5 for a claim that existed at year-end. The controller asks which of these events require financial statement adjustment versus disclosure only under ASC 855. What recommendation should the audit team present?",
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Under ASC 855 (Subsequent Events), events are classified as Type I (recognized subsequent events) or Type II (non-recognized subsequent events). Type I events provide additional evidence about conditions that existed at the balance sheet date and require adjustment of the financial statements. Type II events provide evidence about conditions that arose after the balance sheet date and require disclosure only if material. Event 1 (customer bankruptcy) is Type I: the condition that caused the receivable to become uncollectible (customer financial distress) existed at year-end — the December 31 confirmation only confirmed amounts owed, not future collectibility. Event 3 (lawsuit settlement) is Type I: the underlying claim existed at year-end, and the settlement provides additional evidence about the amount of the year-end liability. Event 2 (warehouse fire) is Type II: the fire damage arose entirely after year-end and provides no evidence about year-end conditions — it requires disclosure only as a material non-recognized event. The audit team should recommend that Events 1 and 3 require adjustment (Type I), and Event 2 requires disclosure only (Type II).",
