    "ExplanationCorrect": "Fixed overhead volume variance = (Budgeted Hours - Standard Hours for Actual Output) x Standard Fixed Overhead Rate. Budgeted hours = 12,000 x 3 = 36,000 DLH. Standard hours for actual output = 11,500 x 3 = 34,500 DLH. Standard fixed overhead rate = $180,000 / 36,000 = $5 per DLH. Volume variance = (36,000 - 34,500) x $5 = 1,500 x $5 = $7,500 unfavorable. Analysis: The unfavorable volume variance signals that Northgate produced 500 fewer units than planned (11,500 vs. 12,000 budgeted), leaving 1,500 DLH of fixed overhead capacity unabsorbed. This $7,500 underapplied fixed overhead must be charged to cost of goods sold, reducing operating income below budget. Critically, the volume variance reflects capacity utilization, not spending control -- the $2,000 actual spending over budget ($182,000 vs. $180,000) is a separate FOH budget variance. Management should investigate whether the production shortfall was caused by demand weakness, machine downtime, or material shortages, and whether the idle capacity is temporary or structural. Compare to standard hours for actual output to isolate capacity utilization from rate changes: the $5/DLH rate is fixed in the short run, so the only actionable lever is production volume.",
