    "Stem": "Harbor View Corporation sells two products. Budget: Product A (40% of mix, CM = $30/unit), Product B (60% of mix, CM = $20/unit). Total budgeted sales = 10,000 units. Actual sales: A = 5,000 units, B = 5,000 units. What is the sales mix variance?",
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Sales mix variance = sum of [(Actual Mix% - Budgeted Mix%) x Actual Total Units x Budgeted CM per Unit]. A: (50% - 40%) x 10,000 x $30 = $30,000 F. B: (50% - 60%) x 10,000 x $20 = -$20,000 U. Total = $30,000 + (-$20,000) = $10,000 favorable. Analysis: The $10,000 favorable mix variance is a quality-of-sales insight -- Harbor View improved profitability not by selling more units overall (total volume stayed at budgeted 10,000), but by shifting its sales composition toward the higher-margin Product A (CM = $30/unit vs. $20 for B). Product A grew from 40% to 50% of mix, while Product B fell from 60% to 50%. Management should assess whether the Product A growth came from marketing investment pricing strategy or competitor weakness -- and whether the B decline signals a market shift or cannibalization. Critically, the sales mix variance must be analyzed together with the sales quantity variance: if total units had also changed, the mix effect could be masked or amplified by pure volume change. The mix variance answers 'did we sell more of the right products?' while the quantity variance answers 'did we sell enough total units?'",
