    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Lakeshore Division has operating income of $200,000. Total assets are $1,000,000, current liabilities are $100,000, and the weighted-average cost of capital is 12%. The tax rate is 30%. What is the economic value added (EVA)?",
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "EVA = After-tax Operating Income - [(Total Assets - Current Liabilities) x WACC]. After-tax OI = $200,000 x (1 - 0.30) = $140,000. Invested capital = $1,000,000 - $100,000 = $900,000. Capital charge = $900,000 x 12% = $108,000. EVA = $140,000 - $108,000 = $32,000. Analysis: The positive $32,000 EVA confirms Lakeshore Division is creating shareholder value -- its return on invested capital (ROCE = $140,000 / $900,000 = 15.6%) exceeds the cost of capital (12%), generating a 3.6% positive spread. Management should interpret a positive EVA as evidence that the division's growth strategy is value-accretive and should continue, while also benchmarking the magnitude: $32,000 on a $900,000 capital base represents a modest 3.6% excess return. Critically, if Lakeshore's EVA were negative, it would signal value destruction even if operating income were positive -- a division can be profitable in accounting terms yet fail to cover its cost of capital. Compare EVA to residual income (RI): EVA = NOPAT - (Invested Capital x WACC), while RI = Operating Income - (Required Return x Assets). EVA adjusts for after-tax income and uses WACC, making it a more realistic measure of economic profit. The manager should also consider whether the current-liability deduction ($100,000) properly reflects invested capital or whether additional adjustments (operating leases, R&D capitalization, LIFO reserves) would change the capital charge calculation.",
    "SourceDescription": "Original CMA Part 1 exam-style practice. Not real CMA exam content and not copied from official samples.",
