    "Difficulty": "Difficult",
    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Westlake Health, a regional hospital system with 8,200 employees and 1.4 million patient records, conducted a tabletop exercise simulating a ransomware attack on its electronic health record (EHR) system. The exercise revealed three critical gaps in the current incident response plan (IRP): (Gap 1) Containment Protocol — the IRP states 'IT will isolate affected systems within 60 minutes of confirmed breach' but the exercise showed the network segmentation architecture cannot isolate the EHR without also disconnecting the radiology PACS and laboratory information system, potentially delaying patient care for up to 80 minutes while manual workarounds are activated; estimated remediation cost $420,000 for network micro-segmentation; (Gap 2) Communication Escalation — the IRP designates the IT Director as the primary incident commander but does not specify an escalation path to the CEO or Board if the IT Director is unavailable or compromised (the exercise scenario assumed the IT Director's credentials were among those compromised); estimated remediation cost $45,000 for crisis communication protocol development and tabletop retesting; (Gap 3) Evidence Preservation — the IRP requires 'preserve forensic images of all affected systems' but the hospital's forensic imaging tools are stored on the same network segment as the EHR and would be encrypted by the ransomware before imaging could begin; estimated remediation cost $185,000 for air-gapped forensic workstation and offline gold-image repository. The CISO, Rachel Tam, must prioritize these three gaps for remediation within a $500,000 annual cybersecurity budget, with the Board requiring that the most critical patient-safety and regulatory (HIPAA) risks be addressed first. Which gap should the CISO remediate first?",
    "CorrectChoice": "D",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Gap 1 (Containment Protocol) is the correct first priority. In incident response, containment is the FIRST and most time-critical phase of the NIST Cybersecurity Framework's Respond function. The exercise revealed that current containment procedures create a direct patient-safety conflict: isolating the EHR to stop ransomware propagation simultaneously disables the radiology and lab systems that clinicians depend on for diagnosis and treatment decisions. During an actual ransomware attack, this architectural flaw forces the incident commander to choose between cybersecurity containment and patient care continuity — an unacceptable clinical risk. The CMA candidate must evaluate that in a healthcare environment, patient safety is the paramount consideration, and an IRP that creates a clinical-care-versus-cybersecurity trade-off during incident response is fundamentally defective. At $420,000, it fits within the annual budget and can be implemented before the next incident occurs. The remaining $80,000 partially funds Gap 2's crisis communication protocol, addressing the command-structure concern at a basic level.",
    "SourceDescription": "Original CMA Part 1 exam-style practice. Not real CMA exam content and not copied from official samples.",
    "QuestionID": "P1B-F-110",
    "question_state": "Certified",
    "ReviewNote": "If missed or marked, review the linked study materials and rework the underlying concept without looking at the answer.",
    "CalculationItem": false,
    "Choices": {
      "A": "All three gaps are equally critical and must be remediated simultaneously; the CISO should request a supplemental budget allocation of $150,000 from the Board to close all three within the fiscal year",
      "B": "Gap 2 (Communication Escalation) — a missing escalation path means the organization could be leaderless during the most critical hours of incident response; at $45,000 it is the cheapest gap to close, leaving $455,000 for the other two gaps; without command structure, containment and evidence preservation become uncoordinated",
      "C": "Gap 3 (Evidence Preservation) — without forensic evidence, Westlake cannot determine the scope of the breach, notify affected patients as required by HIPAA, or support law enforcement investigation; the $185,000 cost leaves $315,000 for Gap 1 remediation",
      "D": "Gap 1 (Containment Protocol) — the inability to isolate the EHR without disrupting radiology and lab systems creates a direct patient-safety risk during incident response; every minute of EHR unavailability during an actual ransomware attack compounds clinical risk, and the $420,000 cost is within the $500,000 budget, leaving $80,000 to partially address Gap 2"
    },
    "StudyLinks": [
      {
        "label": "IMA CMA Learning Outcome Statements, Part 1 Section F",
        "url": "https://prodcm.imanet.org/-/media/IMA/Files/Home/IMA-Certifications/CMA-Certification/2024-CMA-Learning-Outcome-Statement-Final.ashx"
      },
      {
        "label": "OpenStax: Principles of Accounting, Technology and Analytics",
        "url": "https://openstax.org/books/principles-managerial-accounting/pages/1-introduction"
      }
    ],
    "VerifiedChecks": [
      "Mapped to CMA Part 1 Learning Outcome Statements effective September 1, 2024"
    ],
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Requesting a supplemental budget allocation is a reasonable escalation when all gaps are genuinely critical. However, the Board's directive was to prioritize within the $500,000 budget — the CISO was asked to exercise judgment, not to defer the decision upward. Additionally, remediation does not need to be simultaneous to be effective; sequential remediation that addresses the highest patient-safety risk first, then communication, then forensics, produces a continuously improving security posture rather than waiting for budget approval while all three gaps remain open. The governance principle is that risk management requires prioritization when resources are constrained, not a demand for more resources.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Gap 2 (Communication Escalation) is genuinely important — an incident response without clear command authority is dangerously disorganized. At $45,000, it is also the most cost-effective fix. However, a well-communicated incident response that cannot contain the incident is worse than a contained incident with imperfect communication. The escalation protocol enables coordinated decision-making, but if the containment decision itself (disconnect or don't disconnect?) is fundamentally broken because of network architecture, no amount of escalation clarity fixes it. The priority framework is: contain first, communicate second, investigate third. An escalation protocol that simply routes the impossible containment decision up the chain of command does not resolve the underlying patient-safety conflict.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "Gap 3 (Evidence Preservation) is critical for post-incident obligations — HIPAA breach notification, scope determination, and law enforcement support all depend on forensic evidence. However, evidence preservation is a POST-containment activity. The forensic workstation cannot image systems that have not yet been contained. If the ransomware is still propagating, there are no stable forensic images to capture. The incident response sequence under NIST SP 800-61 is: Preparation → Detection & Analysis → Containment, Eradication & Recovery → Post-Incident Activity. Evidence preservation supports the Detection & Analysis and Post-Incident phases, but it cannot be performed effectively until containment is achieved. Priority must follow the chronological dependency of incident response activities.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "",
