    "ExplanationWrongA": "RPA cannot perform the unstructured text classification required by Process 2 — bots follow rules, and there is no deterministic rule for 'this email expresses customer frustration about a late delivery.' Similarly, RPA cannot model the seasonal demand patterns, lead-time variability, and cross-SKU correlations required by Process 3 — bots execute calculations, but they do not discover patterns in data. Applying RPA to processes requiring pattern recognition will produce automation that follows rules but makes wrong decisions. The technology classification principle is that RPA automates execution of known rules; it does not discover rules from data.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Applying AI/ML to all four processes is technically feasible but economically and operationally suboptimal. For Processes 1 and 4, machine learning would be more expensive, harder to explain to auditors (why was THIS invoice flagged as a mismatch?), and less reliable than deterministic rules for tasks that deterministic rules already perform at 93% accuracy. The governance principle is technology proportionality: use the simplest technology that reliably solves the problem. Over-engineering deterministic processes with ML introduces model risk, explainability challenges, and unnecessary cost without corresponding benefit.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "",
