    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Nexus Manufacturing recently implemented an integrated ERP system. During a post-implementation review, the internal auditor discovers that the accounts payable manager has both the ability to create new vendors in the vendor master file and approve purchase orders up to $50,000, while the purchasing manager can modify goods receipt quantities without secondary approval. The CFO asks the auditor to recommend the most effective control remediation. Which recommendation best addresses the segregation of duties risks while minimizing operational disruption?",
    "CorrectChoice": "A",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The most effective remediation configures role-based access controls (RBAC) within the ERP system to separate incompatible duties at the system level, rather than relying on manual oversight. For vendor creation and purchase order approval, these are incompatible duties under COSO Principle 10 — the same person who can create a vendor should not be able to authorize payments to that vendor. Configuring the ERP system to assign these functions to different roles enforces SoD preventively. For goods receipt quantity modifications, a tolerance-based threshold (e.g., variances exceeding 5% require supervisor review) balances control effectiveness with operational efficiency — it allows normal minor adjustments while flagging material deviations. This approach addresses both risks identified by the auditor: the AP manager's combined vendor creation and PO approval access, and the purchasing manager's unmonitored receipt quantity modifications. The recommendation preserves the ERP system's operational benefits while embedding preventive controls at the configuration layer, consistent with COSO Principle 11's requirement that control activities be deployed through policies and procedures that are built into business processes and technology. A common error is to recommend abandoning the ERP workflow entirely — this sacrifices integration benefits without addressing the underlying control design issue.",
