    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Summit Manufacturing's finance team has been piloting a generative AI tool (a large language model deployed on a private cloud instance) to draft management discussion and analysis (MD&A) sections, variance explanations for the monthly CFO package, and contract summary memos for the legal department. The pilot processed 124 documents in Q3, and the finance team estimates a 40% reduction in drafting time. However, the Controller identified three incidents during the pilot: (Incident 1) the model generated a variance explanation attributing a $2.1M favorable materials variance to 'supplier renegotiation' when the actual cause was a temporary commodity price decline — the explanation was factually plausible but wrong; (Incident 2) a contract summary memo included a vendor's confidential pricing terms that were contained in the source contract but should not have been reproduced in a summary circulated beyond the procurement team; (Incident 3) the model responded to a 'draft a Q4 forecast narrative' prompt by incorporating Q4 data from a DIFFERENT division's uploaded spreadsheet, producing a coherent but entirely fabricated forecast. The CFO, David Okonkwo, has asked the Controller to evaluate two control frameworks before approving the tool for production use: (Framework 1) Human-in-the-Loop Mandate — every AI-generated output must be reviewed, fact-checked, and approved by a qualified finance professional before dissemination; outputs carry a 'Generated by AI — Human Reviewed' watermark; all source data uploaded to the model must be approved by a data steward; estimated additional review burden: 6.5 hours/week across the finance team, $78,000 annualized cost for review time, no additional technology investment; (Framework 2) Automated Guardrails + Sampling Review — deploy content filtering rules (prohibiting reproduction of marked-confidential text, enforcing division-level data isolation, requiring source attribution for every factual claim), combined with weekly random sampling (15% of outputs) reviewed by internal audit; estimated technology cost $240,000 for guardrail deployment, review burden 2 hours/week for internal audit sampling; risk: automated guardrails may not catch the full range of hallucination patterns. The Audit Committee has directed that the framework must prevent the three documented incident types from recurring. Which framework should the Controller recommend?",
    "CorrectChoice": "C",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "Framework 1 (Human-in-the-Loop Mandate) is the correct recommendation. Generative AI hallucinations — plausible-sounding but factually incorrect outputs — are a known limitation of large language models that no current automated guardrail technology can fully eliminate. The three incidents demonstrate this: a commodity-price vs. renegotiation confusion requires subject-matter knowledge of Summit's supplier contracts that no content filter possesses; confidential pricing reproduction requires understanding of information classification that is context-dependent; cross-division data contamination requires knowledge of Summit's organizational structure. Human review is the only control that can apply professional judgment across all three failure modes. At $78,000 annually, the cost is substantially lower than Framework 2's $240,000 capital outlay, and the 6.5 hours/week is proportionate to the risks: financial reporting errors in MD&A or variance explanations can have regulatory consequences under SEC Rule 10b-5. The AI governance principle tested is that for financial reporting use cases, human accountability cannot be delegated to automated controls.",
