    "ExplanationWrongA": "Terminating the pilot is an overreaction. The 40% reduction in drafting time represents real productivity gains that can be captured IF controls are applied. The question is not whether generative AI can be used — it is HOW it should be controlled. Every new technology introduction in accounting (spreadsheets in the 1980s, ERP systems in the 1990s, cloud computing in the 2010s) involved an initial period of control gaps followed by governance maturation. The appropriate response to documented failures is to design and implement controls, not to abandon the technology. The governance principle is that risk management means controlling technology risk, not avoiding technology.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Automated guardrails are genuinely valuable as a defense-in-depth layer, and the 2-hour/week sampling burden is attractive. However, guardrails fail on the three documented incident types: (1) content filtering cannot distinguish 'supplier renegotiation' from 'commodity price decline' — both are plausible variance explanations; (2) confidential-text detection requires the system to understand Summit's information classification taxonomy, which is a knowledge-management challenge separate from the AI deployment; (3) data isolation by division requires perfect metadata tagging of every uploaded file, which Summit's current document management system does not support. Deploying guardrails that cannot prevent the KNOWN failure modes while reducing human review is decreasing, not increasing, control effectiveness. The governance principle is that automation of controls that cannot perform the required detection function creates a false sense of security.",
