    "ItemType": "MCQ",
    "ItemStyle": "single-select",
    "Stem": "Meridian Insurance migrated its actuarial modeling application to AWS 14 months ago. The application processes policyholder data (including PII such as Social Security numbers, medical histories, and financial information) to calculate insurance premiums. Last week, a security researcher notified Meridian that a publicly accessible S3 bucket contained 47,000 unencrypted policyholder records, including SSNs and medical data, indexed by Google and discoverable through simple search queries. The IT Risk Analyst, Daniel Park, has been asked to analyze the incident under the cloud shared responsibility model. The investigation timeline reveals: (Event 1) 14 months ago — the actuarial team migrated the application to AWS, creating an S3 bucket to store model output files for sharing with external actuarial consultants; the DevOps engineer configured the bucket with 'Block Public Access = OFF' and an S3 bucket policy allowing 'GetObject' for the consultants' IP range; (Event 2) 8 months ago — an AWS Trusted Advisor notification warned that the S3 bucket had 'publicly accessible permissions'; the notification was sent to a distribution list that the DevOps engineer (who left the company 10 months ago) had configured and was not monitored by any active team member; (Event 3) 5 months ago — a routine external penetration test commissioned by Meridian did not include cloud-storage enumeration in its scope; (Event 4) Last week — the security researcher discovered the exposed bucket. AWS's shared responsibility model states that AWS is responsible for 'security OF the cloud' (physical infrastructure, network, hypervisor) and the customer is responsible for 'security IN the cloud' (data classification, access controls, firewall configuration, encryption). Under this model, where does the responsibility for this breach fall?",
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The responsibility falls on Meridian (the customer). Under the AWS shared responsibility model, the customer is responsible for: (1) configuring S3 bucket access controls — the DevOps engineer explicitly configured 'Block Public Access = OFF' and set bucket policies, making this a customer-controlled configuration decision; (2) classifying data and applying appropriate controls — storing unencrypted SSNs and medical histories is a customer data classification decision (AWS has no visibility into the content of customer objects); (3) monitoring and responding to AWS security advisories — the Trusted Advisor notification was generated BY AWS to ALERT the customer to a misconfiguration, satisfying AWS's notification responsibility; the fact that Meridian did not have a monitored distribution list is a customer operational failure. The cloud governance principle tested is that the shared responsibility model is a contractual and operational boundary: the customer cannot delegate data classification, access control configuration, or security advisory response to the cloud provider — these remain the customer's responsibility regardless of the service model (IaaS, PaaS, or SaaS).",
