    "Stem": "Crescent Analytics, a consumer insights firm, is developing a predictive model that analyzes customer transaction data from 14 retail partners to forecast individual purchasing behavior and creditworthiness. The model ingests: purchase history (SKU-level detail for 3 years), geolocation data (store visits, dwell time), payment method patterns (credit vs. debit vs. cash frequency), and publicly available social media activity. The Chief Data Officer, Amara Singh, has been asked by the Board to evaluate the model against three data ethics frameworks before the scheduled launch: (Framework 1) FAT (Fairness, Accountability, Transparency) — requires a fairness audit demonstrating the model does not produce disparate impact across protected demographic categories (the model does not directly ingest demographic data but geolocation and purchase patterns are known proxies for race, income, and gender); requires a publicly documented accountability structure naming the individual responsible for model governance; requires transparency in the form of plain-language disclosures to consumers about what data is used and how predictions affect them; compliance cost $320,000 for fairness audit, disclosure infrastructure, and governance documentation; (Framework 2) IEEE 7000-2021 (Value-Based Engineering) — requires stakeholder value elicitation (structured interviews with consumers, retail partners, regulators, and advocacy groups to identify ethical values and concerns), value-based design requirements integrated into the model development lifecycle, and traceability from ethical values to system requirements; estimated timeline adds 5 months to launch, cost $480,000 including stakeholder engagement and design iteration; (Framework 3) EU Ethics Guidelines for Trustworthy AI — requires the model satisfy seven requirements: human agency and oversight, technical robustness and safety, privacy and data governance, transparency, diversity/non-discrimination/fairness, societal and environmental well-being, and accountability; compliance cost $610,000 with a projected 8-month delay for full conformity assessment; the framework's requirement for 'explicit, informed consent for each specific purpose of data processing' conflicts with Crescent's current data-sharing agreements, which use broad-purpose consent language. The Board has directed that the selected framework must be defensible to consumer advocacy groups AND the retail partners whose data Crescent processes. Which framework should the CDO recommend?",
