    "VerifiedChecks": [
      "Mapped to CMA Part 1 Learning Outcome Statements effective September 1, 2024"
    ],
    "ExplanationWrongA": "Node governance IS a genuine adoption blocker. A permissioned blockchain's entire value proposition — distributed trust among consortium members — collapses if more than half the consortium cannot participate as validator nodes. Providing IT support is a mitigation, not a resolution: IT support cannot fix the 14 cooperatives without reliable internet (the 95% uptime requirement is a technical constraint, not a training gap), and deploying IT staff to 20 remote cooperative locations across two West African countries is a $1.2-1.8M annual operational cost that the feasibility assessment did not include in its budget. Mitigations that cost more than the problem they solve are not viable mitigations — they transform a technical blocker into a financial blocker.",
    "ExplanationWrongB": "Finding 1 (850 TPS capacity vs. 9,200 daily transactions) is clearly NOT a blocker — the blockchain has orders of magnitude more capacity than required. Finding 2 ($24,000 annual energy cost) is similarly NOT a blocker when it represents 0.03% of a $78M program budget. Classifying these as blockers misinterprets the quantitative evidence. The CMA candidate must analyze that the first two findings are TEST RESULTS showing the technology passes feasibility thresholds, not findings showing failure. The distinction between 'finding' (an observation from the assessment, which may be positive, negative, or neutral) and 'blocker' (a finding that prevents project viability) is the analytical skill being tested.",
    "ExplanationWrongC": "While the off-chain-data/on-chain-hash workaround for GDPR is commonly proposed in blockchain literature, it has not been validated by EU regulators or courts. The European Data Protection Board's 2021 guidelines on the right to erasure state that 'where personal data are stored in a blockchain, the erasure of such data should be carried out in a manner that renders the personal data beyond use.' Hashing personal data and storing the hash on-chain while deleting the original data off-chain has not been established as 'beyond use' under this standard. For Verde's sustainability program — which is public-facing and markets ethical sourcing — adopting a legally uncertain privacy workaround is incompatible with the program's trust-building purpose. Additionally, classifying all findings as addressable ignores the 20 cooperatives that cannot function as node operators, which is a practical operational blocker regardless of the legal-regulatory question.",
